    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok that makes sense. so basically it makes it so they cant just use one big list to crack everything at once. i guess i just dont get the name \"salt\" though lol. it's a weird term to use for data. why call it that specifically?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the etymology of \"salt\" is a bit like a mischievous goblin hiding in a dictionary! You're absolutely right that it prevents those big, pre-computed rainbow tables from working on a single pass. But why \"salt\"?\n\nWell, in the old days, people actually *did* put literal salt in their food to preserve it and stop bacteria from growing. It's a way of making the food unique and harder for spoilage (or in this case, attackers) to predict. If you put salt in a stew, no two bowls are exactly the same because the distribution is random. That's exactly what a salt does for your password hash—it makes every single hash unique, just like a bowl of stew with salt in it is different from another bowl, even if the meat and veggies are the same.\n\nSo, the term comes from the idea of \"seasoning\" or \"preserving\" the data to stop it from being easily reused. It's a bit like a goblin hiding in a jar of pickles; you can't just grab the whole jar and eat the goblin without opening it first. The salt is that little goblin hiding in your data, ensuring that even if the attacker",
